(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-pharm-drill-antibacterials"]={"title":"Antibacterials Drill","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which drug causes body flushing, fever and chills during a rapid infusion — Red Man Syndrome?","choices":["Vancomycin","Linezolid","Clindamycin","Gentamicin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Red Man Syndrome, also called Vancomycin Infusion Syndrome. It is an infusion-rate reaction, not an allergy, so the answer is to slow the infusion. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which antibiotic class causes tendonitis and Achilles tendon rupture?","choices":["Macrolides","Fluoroquinolones","Tetracyclines","Aminoglycosides"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tendonitis and Achilles rupture, with caution under 18 years old. Peripheral neuropathy belongs to the same class. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which antibiotic causes a disulfiram-like reaction if the patient drinks alcohol?","choices":["Clindamycin","Vancomycin","Metronidazole","Linezolid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A disulfiram-like reaction with ethanol — the patient must be told not to drink. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"Which antibiotic can cause serotonin syndrome with selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors?","choices":["Vancomycin","Metronidazole","Tigecycline","Linezolid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Serotonin syndrome with SSRIs, and it also interacts with tyramine-containing foods and pseudoephedrine. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"Seizures are the warning attached to which antibiotic class, and to imipenem in particular?","choices":["Carbapenems","Cephalosporins","Monobactams","Penicillins"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Seizures, specifically with imipenem. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which class discolours the teeth and depresses skeletal growth?","choices":["Macrolides","Tetracyclines","Fluoroquinolones","Aminoglycosides"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tooth discolouration, doxycycline more than the others, and depression of skeletal growth. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which antibiotic is the one classically named for Clostridium difficile and pseudomembranous colitis?","choices":["Aztreonam","Linezolid","Clindamycin","Vancomycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Clostridium difficile and pseudomembranous colitis, plus rash, neutropenia and thrombocytopenia. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"Which class prolongs the QT interval and risks Torsades de pointes, worse with class Ia and III antiarrhythmics?","choices":["Penicillins","Aminoglycosides","Carbapenems","Macrolides"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. QT prolongation and Torsades de pointes, and the combination with class Ia and III antiarrhythmics makes it worse. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Renal toxicity and ototoxicity together point to which class?","choices":["Aminoglycosides","Macrolides","Cephalosporins","Clindamycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both at once — the kidney and the eighth nerve. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Stevens-Johnson syndrome with thrombocytopenia and agranulocytosis is the warning on which agent?","choices":["Amoxicillin / clavulanate","Trimethoprim / sulfamethoxazole","Piperacillin / tazobactam","Nitrofurantoin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hypersensitivity through to Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis, plus blood dyscrasias. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"Which class carries black box warnings for nephrotoxicity, neurotoxicity and neuromuscular blockade?","choices":["Aminoglycosides","Vancomycin","Polymyxins","Tigecycline"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Polymyxin B and E (colistin) — all three warnings, and use is rising because of multidrug resistance. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Which antibiotic can be given to a genuinely penicillin-allergic patient because it has no cross-reactivity with beta-lactams?","choices":["Cefazolin","Meropenem","Ampicillin","Aztreonam"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. No cross-reactivity with the beta-lactams, so it is usable in true penicillin allergy. Gram-negative cover only. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which penicillin group was designed solely to cover methicillin-sensitive Staphylococcus aureus?","choices":["Nafcillin","Trimethoprim / sulfamethoxazole","Ampicillin","Piperacillin/tazobactam"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Designed solely for MSSA — and monitored for hepatic rather than renal toxicity, the exception among the beta-lactams. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which is the drug of choice for syphilis, gas gangrene and meningococcus?","choices":["Doxycycline","Penicillin G","Azithromycin","Ceftriaxone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Drug of choice for all three. Good against gram-positives, with no staphylococcal cover. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which is the drug of choice for neutropenic fever?","choices":["Cefazolin","Ceftriaxone","Cefepime","Cephalexin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The fourth-generation cephalosporin, drug of choice for neutropenic fever. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which cephalosporin cannot be used during the first thirty days of life?","choices":["Cefotaxime","Cefazolin","Cefepime","Ceftriaxone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Not in the first 30 days of life; cefotaxime is preferred for neonatal fever or sepsis. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Penicillin cross-sensitivity of roughly less than one per cent belongs to which class?","choices":["Cephalosporins","Carbapenems","Monobactams","Macrolides"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cross-sensitivity of approximately less than 1%, and mostly renally eliminated. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which agent significantly raises the international normalised ratio in a patient on warfarin, and causes hypoglycaemia with sulfonylureas?","choices":["Amoxicillin / clavulanate","Trimethoprim / sulfamethoxazole","Piperacillin / tazobactam","Clindamycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both interactions belong to it, which is why the warfarin patient needs closer monitoring. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"Which class must be avoided in the second and third trimesters of pregnancy?","choices":["Penicillins","Cephalosporins","Tetracyclines","Macrolides"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Avoided in the second and third trimesters, and in children, because of tooth discolouration and skeletal effects. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Transient hearing loss and cholestatic hepatitis belong to which class?","choices":["Aminoglycosides","Tetracyclines","Fluoroquinolones","Macrolides"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both, alongside the gastrointestinal upset that is worst with erythromycin. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which agent does NOT cover vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus?","choices":["Tigecycline","Linezolid","Vancomycin","Clindamycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Covers complicated skin and intra-abdominal infection but NOT VRE — the exception worth remembering. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Which is the drug of choice for polymicrobial and nosocomial infections, especially pneumonia and intra-abdominal sepsis?","choices":["Amoxicillin/clavulanate","Piperacillin/tazobactam","Cefazolin","Aztreonam"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Drug of choice for polymicrobial and nosocomial infection, including hospital pneumonia. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which is the drug of choice for skin and soft tissue infection, diabetic foot, and animal or human bites?","choices":["Piperacillin/tazobactam","Ceftriaxone","Amoxicillin/clavulanate","Metronidazole"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Drug of choice for exactly those, because the clavulanate covers the mouth flora in a bite. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which class is the drug of choice for multidrug-resistant gram-negative and extended-spectrum beta-lactamase infections?","choices":["Aminopenicillins","First-generation cephalosporins","Macrolides","Carbapenems"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Drug of choice for MDR gram-negatives and ESBL producers. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which antibiotic covers gram-negative organisms only?","choices":["Aztreonam","Vancomycin","Ceftaroline","Piperacillin/tazobactam"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Gram-negative only — and no cross-reactivity with the other beta-lactams. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which fluoroquinolone must not be used for a urinary tract infection and has no Pseudomonas cover?","choices":["Levofloxacin","Moxifloxacin","Ciprofloxacin","Ofloxacin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Not for urinary tract infection, and no Pseudomonas cover — levofloxacin is the one with it. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"Which class is the drug of choice for febrile neutropenia, sepsis and enterococcal synergy?","choices":["Macrolides","Tetracyclines","Aminoglycosides","Clindamycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Drug of choice for all three, which is why the renal and ear monitoring matters. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which class causes photosensitivity and complexes with cations such as antacids and dairy?","choices":["Macrolides","Penicillins","Clindamycin","Fluoroquinolones"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Photosensitivity, and they chelate with cations — so they must be separated from antacids, iron and dairy. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which is the drug of choice for Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia, both to treat and to prevent it?","choices":["Trimethoprim / sulfamethoxazole","Clindamycin with primaquine","Azithromycin","Fluconazole"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Treatment AND prophylaxis, which is why it is used long term in immunosuppressed patients. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"Which cephalosporin generation carries MRSA cover?","choices":["Cefazolin","Ceftaroline","Ceftriaxone","Cefepime"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The fifth generation, used for skin and soft tissue infection WITH MRSA cover — the only cephalosporin that has it. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 37"}]};
